Z||000004||Jncomutt||09-09-2001||09:11 PM||||Thank You MrPBody, thats what I was looking for.  I'm going to get the old one off of the parts car tomorrow.  I thought that adding a rear sway bar would help to reduce understeer??  The site says otherwise, guess I have to look to upgrading the front as well... <P>Now.. I remember hearing about some guy who makes something and everyone had nothing but the highest compliments of him.  He also designed and sells the 'Strut-tower brace"  I had his email saved but lost it in the computer crash.  The brace sold for $120, someone has to know who I'm talking about.  <P>Thanks again!!<P>EDIT:  The things the guy made were TBs.  His name is Darrel (morse86@aol.com)  Came across it by accident in an unexpected search string.  <p>[This message has been edited by Jncomutt (edited 09-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||09-10-2001||07:35 AM||||Adding a rear bar will reduce understeer when you're cornering fairly hard, by changing the balance of forces acting on front and rear tires.  However, Fiero understeers <I>not</I> because the front tires are too heavily loaded, but I think because the outside front tire leans outward under cornering loads, losing grip.  Stiffer anti-roll bars help this indirectly by limiting body roll in cornering.  Lowering helps, too.<P>To complement the rear bar, you really should install a heavier front bar.  I used the front bar from Fiero Store; it came with urethane links and bushings.<P>For better explanation, see "Tech Topics" at <A HREF="http://www.grmotorsports.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.grmotorsports.com/</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||ray b||09-10-2001||11:51 AM||||I don't think that you need a tower brace.<BR>fiero's are stiff and don't flex much. unless you make cuts or remove the trunk.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
