Z||000011||artherd||09-10-2001||08:19 PM||||Yeah, they really don't do anything in Fieros (check out the HUGE chassis member that is welded to both shock towers and runs through the trunk...)<P>But hey, they look allright, if a bit silly in Fieros. (kinda like how pontiac ran the heater core lines all the way to the BACK of the car, just to have them join the main pipes juuust before they go to rubber and up to the engine... it's like "why?" and stuff?:P<P>The whole 'front bar on rear' thing always seemed like a really poor kludge at best, and very very dangerous at worst.  I'd recomend a good aftermarket bar, they're really not that expensive for god's sake!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000012||KRMFiero||09-10-2001||08:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Yeah, they really don't do anything in Fieros (check out the HUGE chassis member that is welded to both shock towers and runs through the trunk...)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>and that chassis member is VERY strong, thats what I use to lift the Back of my Fiero up when I use my chain hoist  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||MrPBody||09-10-2001||09:09 PM||||artherd --<P>I used to share your suspicion; however, there's "dangerous kludge" and then there's "creative adaptation."  Having done it, I classify front-bar-on-rear as the latter.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Jncomutt||09-10-2001||10:30 PM||||So would you guys recommend that I don't perform this mod???  I'm open to all ideas, thats why I created this thread before I started.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Will||09-10-2001||10:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>The whole 'front bar on rear' thing always seemed like a really poor kludge at best, and very very dangerous at worst.  I'd recomend a good aftermarket bar, they're really not that expensive for god's sake!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I disagree. There's absolutely no difference between a stock Fiero bar of 7/8" diameter and effective lever arm X inches long and an aftermarket bar of 7/8" diameter and effective lever arm X inches in length.<BR>Adding a swaybar is nothing more than an exercise in critical driving. Install bar, drive, decide if you like the new balance or if it needs something else.<P>I have rod-end swaybar links all around with a WS6 front bar in the rear of my '87 GT. I have the links set up using '88 Fiero end link brackets to make the bar direct acting on the hub carriers, instead of connected 2/3 of the way out on the control arm.<P>In other words, I have effectively 40% more rear bar stiffness than the method in the above article gives, but I still lke the balance: a touch of oversteer and a marvelous lift-off reaction easily corrected by throttle application. Kids, don't try this at home.<P>I recently got a ticket for coming off a ramp marked "max safe speed 25 mph" at 70 mph. It handles pretty well.||||reg||1
